ID  - SG-086B7F502E0C
TI  - A review of fluid–solid interaction experimental techniques: A comprehensive classification, development, and future directions in geo-energy science
AU  - Xuguang Dai
AU  - Lei Yang
AU  - Yu Song
AU  - Shuxun Sang
AU  - Meng Wang
AU  - Sijian Zheng
AU  - Xiaofei Liu
AU  - Xiaoran Wang
AU  - Weimin Kong
AU  - Guangjun Feng
AU  - Chongtao Wei
AU  - Zhuang Ma
AU  - Veerle Vandeginste
JO  - Physics of Fluids
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.1063/5.0301108
AB  - Laboratory-based experimental equipment provides valuable insights into the physicochemical interactions among gas, liquid, and rock in surface and subsurface environments. They help elucidate key processes such as enhanced hydrocarbon recovery, fluid–solid reactions, and CO2 sequestration. This work reviews experimental equipment used to study gas–liquid–rock interactions at varying reservoir conditions. It classifies the equipment by structural design, operating conditions, and functionality. A comparative analysis is conducted to evaluate their performance, strengths, limitations, and the technical challenges currently faced in the field. Static and stirred equipment are suitable for elucidating ex situ material migration characteristics, while core flooding equipment excels in replicating in situ fluid transport and fluid–solid interactions. Recent technical cases indicate that the utilization and development of these setups have gained widespread attention, and certain progress has been made in experimental techniques. Besides the overview of existing experimental techniques, this review introduces an innovative in situ dynamic core flooding setup featuring gas–liquid premixing and injection under controlled temperature, pressure, and stress fields. The equipment is capable of providing injection pressures of 0–65 MPa, confining pressures of 0–75 MPa, and temperatures of 0–250 °C. It adopts a premixing injection approach inspired by the Iceland CarbFix project, ensuring complete gas–liquid mixing and thereby substantially improving interaction efficiency. The findings indicate that developing more realistic and efficient experimental systems is essential for advancing research and innovation in geo-energy science.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0301108
