TI  - The role and significance of extracellular polymers in activated sludge. Part I: Literature review
AU  - Anna Raszka
AU  - Monika Chorvatova
AU  - Jir̆í Wanner
JO  - Acta hydrochimica et hydrobiologica
PY  - 2006
DO  - 10.1002/aheh.200500640
AB  - In biological wastewater treatment, bacteria tend to aggregate by forming flocs, biofilms and even granules. A large part of the floc structure in activated sludge is composed of extracellular polymers. The extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) are mainly responsible for the structural and functional integrity of biofilms/flocs and they are considered to be important for the physico-chemical properties of activated sludge. This review considers the composition of EPS and proportion between the constituents. Moreover it includes the relationship between the EPS constituents and sludge properties such as sludge retention time, hydrophobicity and surface charge.
