TI  - Activated Sludge and Other Aerobic Suspended Culture Processes
AU  - Timothy G. Ellis
AU  - Christopher G. Schmit
AU  - Boris Eliosov
AU  - Kauser Jahan
JO  - Water Environment Research
PY  - 2001
DO  - 10.2175/106143001x143529
AB  - Models.Two of the activated sludge models within STOAT, a whole plant modeling program developed at the Water Research Centre, were compared and shown to have definite differences in predictions (Stokes et al., 2000a).The International Water Association, IWA, (formerly IAWQ) Activated Sludge Model No. 1 (ASM1) within STOAT was shown to provide better predictions over a wider range of process conditions and for optimizing nitrification/denitrification than the biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) based ASAL model.The sludge yield predictions were also different for the two models (Stokes et al., 2000b), and the authors suggested that predicted values in ASAL were influenced by the mixing regime selected for the aeration tank.Respirometric measurements were used to determine the readily degradable influent chemical oxygen demand (COD) of Swiss municipal wastewater during determination of kinetic and stoichiometric parameters required for the calibration of ASM3 (Koch et al., 2000).The calibrated parameter set allowed successful modeling of waste activated sludge production and denitrification rates.Brenner (2000) used the IWA Task Group's recommendations and AQUASIM to calibrate and validate a model for biological nitrogen and phosphorus removal in a sequencing batch reactor (SBR).The model required dynamic calibration of 15 process rate equations employing 40 different kinetic and stoichiometric parameters.Batch tests proved helpful for characterizing the biological nutrient (N and P) removal sludge and calibration of a revised integrated model Copyrightã 2001 by the Water Environment Federation.Water Environment Research, Volume 73, Number 5 Literature Review 2001.describing aerobic and anoxic reactions (Brdjanovic et al., 2000).The model required calibration of 3 of 60 default parameters, utilized the COD and N conversion modules from ASM2, and replaced the equations for P removal with the Delft bio-P model.Modeling and laboratory experiments showed that effluent soluble COD from nitrifying systems increased in proportion to influent ammonia concentrations as a results of incomplete biodegradation by heterotrophs of soluble microbial products produced by autotrophs (de Silva and Rittmann, 2000b).The nitrifiers slow response to loading changes on the system dictated the time required to achieve the next steady state.The dynamic performance of an activated sludge system in response to a shock load of glucose (300 g/L) was modeled using Mantis, a model derived from ASM1 (Ellis et al., 2000).Model results showed conditions conducive to filamentous bulking, such as oxygen limitation, were likely to result.A modified Newton-Raphson technique was employed to simultaneously solve the differential equations for a tanks-in-series activated sludge model describing the performance with respect to mixed liquor, dissolved oxygen, biochemical oxygen demand (BOD), and clarifier effluent solids concentrations (Puteh, et al., 1999).The model incorporated elements for the biological reactions occurring in the aeration basin and the settling reactions in the clarifier.Four case studies were used to evaluate a steady-state model based on the Water Environment Federation -American Society of Civil Engineers (WEF-ASCE) model describing carbonaceous and nitrogenous BOD removal in attached and suspended growth systems (Gabaldón, et al., 2000).The four case studies demonstrate the applicability to both large and small plants and municipal and industrial wastewaters.A multispecies model was developed and applied to the SBR treatment of wastewater (de Silva and Rittmann, 2000a).The dynamic model predicted the concentrations of 12 species, including soluble microbial products, which were required to track changes in soluble COD, biomass, and nitrogen.
