AB  - Models.The IA WQ Task Group's Activated Sludge Model No.2 (ASM2) was introduced by Gujer et al. (1995).Its development offers an improvement over Activated Sludge Model No.1 (ASM I) by including a term for phosphorus accumulating organisms in the simulation of biological phosphorus removal processes.ASM2 was used to explain a rise in phosphate levels due to an accumulation of polyhydroxyalkanoates in an alternating type activated sludge pilot plant (Isaacs et al., 1995).The addition of a reaction term describing the rate of denitrification by phosphate accumulating organisms allowed the model to more accurately describe the data.In another study, three submodels to ASM2 were proposed to account for glycogen storage and anoxic uptake of soluble orthophosphate (Mino et aI., 1995b).This study also characterized a group of organisms causing deterioration of phosphate removal.Dold and Briggs (1995) extended the activated sludge process model to include chemical precipitation of phosphorus.Using data from full-scale municipal wastewater treatment plants, their results suggest that nitrifying activated sludge plants which practice chemical precipitation of phosphorus generate as much as 48% more sludge than plants with enhanced biological phosphorus removal.Using pilot plant data, Barbeau et al. (l995b) developed a model to predict the removal of phosphorus in an activated sludge plant with enhanced biological phosphorus removal.This model led to the development and pilot testing of an alternative process configuration which reduced capital expenditures.Application of the activated sludge model with and without biological phosphorus removal at thirteen full-scale wastewater treatment plants highlighted the ability of the model to accurately predict nitrogen removal (Daigger and Nolasco, 1995).Results suggest that more work is needed to account for the effects ofbioreactor configuration and oxygen transfer system as well as the dynamic aspects of biological phosphorus removal.Smolders et al. (1995a, b, c, d) proposed a structured metabolic model to describe the dynamic behavior of storage compounds in the anaerobic and aerobic zones of the biological phosphorus removal process.The model incorporates the concentrations of dissolved acetate, phosphate, and ammonium, oxygen consumption, poly-13-hydroxybutyrate, glycogen and active biomass.In another biological phosphorus removal model, model assumptions and the interaction between the phosphorus accumulating and nitrogen eliminating organisms were considered in light of recent literature (Ante et aI., 1994).The effect of biochemical reactions occurring in the collection system on wastewater treatment plant performance was simulated using a model incorporating elements for combined and sanitary sewerage systems (Gall et at., 1995).The
