AB  - sludge plant in Australia, was investigated (Carr et al., 2004).The strain was found to have two ISR lengths, 682 and 591-593 bp, based on polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplification.These two ISRs were cloned, sequenced, and aligned.Conserved and variable regions were identified, and the implications for ISR fingerprinting to distinguish between strains within a species were discussed.A DNA microarray was generated using plasmid-based 16S-23S rRNA ISRs to analyze microbial community composition in industrial wastewater treatment plants (Cook et al., 2004).Hybridization to the array spots was highly specific and strong fluorescence intensity corresponded to nearperfect match clones.A Cy-5 labeled reference standard was included that matched the spotted plasmid sequence, to normalize hybridization values.The effect of nonylphenol ethoxylate surfactants on the bacterial diversity in lab-scale activated sludge reactors was investigated using fluorescence in-situ hybridization (FISH) and dot blot hybridization.The molecular techniques showed dominance of clusters composed of Betaproteobacteria, accounting for up to one-third of DAPI-stained cells in amended reactors and only 5% of DAPI-stained cells in the controls (Lozada et al., 2004).FISH with DNA probes targeting 16S rRNA genes was performed to analyze the nitrifying microbial communities in an activated sludge and a fixed-biofilm reactor for piggery wastewater treatment.The analysis showed that ammonia-oxidizing bacteria (AOB) (with the DNA probe NSO1225) and denitrifying bacteria (RRP1088) were less abundant than other bacteria (EUB338) in the activated sludge system.Further analysis on nitrifying bacteria in the biofilm system showed that Nitrosomonas species (NSM156) and Nitrospira species (NSR1156) were the dominant ammonia-oxidizing and nitriteoxidizing bacteria, respectively (Kim et al., 2004b).Pectinatus portalensis nov.sp., a 15547531,
