AB  - General.A comprehensive review of the ecology, physiology, biochemistry, and genetics of methanogenic bacteria was published (Ferry, 1993).Biesterveld et al. (l994a) found that the anaerobic hemicellulolytic bacterium, Bacteroides xylanolyticus X5-1, did not consume xylose in the presence of glucose but rather a xylose catabolic pathway was induced after the addition of xylose to a glucose-limited continuous culture.The xylose metabolism of B. xylanolyticus X5-1 was studied by determining specific enzyme activities.The pentose phosphate pathway and Embden-Meyerhof-Parnas pathway were combined for xylose fermentation to pyruvate (Biesterveld et al., 1994b).The fermentation of xylose by the strictly anaerobic hemicellulolytic organism Bacteroides xylanolyticus X5-1 in the presence of CO, H2' and different external electron acceptors (dihydroxyacetone, acetone, acetonin, and acetol) was studied (Biesterveld et at., 1994c).The uptake of pentose sugars by Ruminococcus albus was driven by ATP hydrolysis and metabolism occurred through the pentose phosphate pathway (Thurston et at., 1994).The carbohydrate fermentation was found in Thermotoga maritima.an anaerobic hyperthermophilic bacterium that grows at 90°C, and glucose was fermented to acetate, CO2, and H2 (Blarney and Adams, 1994;Schroder et at., 1994).The pathways involved in this metabolic process were studied in detail and the specific enzymes were identified.Clostridium grantii sp.nov., a new bacterium was isolated from muIlet gut and its optimum growth conditions were established (Mountfort et al., 1994).Abrini et at. (1994) proposed the name Clostridium autoethanogenum for an anaerobic Grampositive strain that metabolizes carbon monoxide to ethanol, acetate, and CO2• The degradation of the 4Fe-4S clusters of ferredoxin in Methanosarcina thermophila during methanogenesis from acetate was studied by Clements et at.(1994).Sauer et at.(1994) observed that the proton motive force necessary for the synthesis of ATP in Methanobacterium thermoautotophicum was developed due to Na+ extrusion and a pH gradient that was generated during methanogenesis.Megasphaera elsdenii strain NAlH 1102 was found to fement lactate preferentially over glucose and to convert it to propionate.Glucose utilization did not resume until lactate concentrations had been depleted to approximately 1-2 mM (Hino et al., 1994).Yu et al. (1994) reported that glycogen appears to be catabolized by the Emhden-Meyerhoff-Parnas pathway by Methanococcus maripaludis.a
