ID  - SG-FB624ED69581
TI  - Anaerobic Digestion: Principles, Mechanisms and Applications
AU  - Nadege Mugisha
AU  - Isha Bodhe
AU  - Velvizhi Gokuladoss
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1021/bk-2026-1534.ch001
AB  - Abstract Anaerobic digestion (AD) is a four-stage microbial process that converts organic matter into biogas and digestate under oxygen-free conditions, playing a critical role in sustainable waste management, renewable energy generation, and nutrient recycling in a circular bioeconomy. Its growing relevance stems from the urgent global demand for low-carbon technologies capable of valorising diverse waste streams. This chapter provides an integrated analysis of AD, linking its biochemical foundations with engineering design and process optimisation strategies. Key aspects addressed include feedstock stoichiometry and compositional influence on methane yield, syntrophic microbial interactions, thermodynamic and kinetic constraints, and reactor configurations. The role of mathematical modelling in bridging laboratory-scale understanding with industrial-scale implementation is highlighted, establishing AD as a scientifically rigorous and technologically adaptable platform for advancing sustainable waste management and resource recovery.
