TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-042FC954C351
TI  - Deciphering inhibitor dynamics and valorization pathways in Eichhornia crassipes biorefineries: Beyond the fermentative hurdle
AU  - Nawaf S. Alhajeri
AU  - Ahmed Tawfik
JO  - Journal of Engineering Research
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.jer.2026.10.005
AB  - Eichhornia crassipes (water hyacinth) has attracted growing attention as a sustainable feedstock for biofuel production and resource recovery due to its rapid growth, high carbohydrate content, and invasive nature. However, large-scale commercialization remains constrained by biomass recalcitrance, pretreatment-induced inhibitor formation, and inadequate integration of conversion pathways. This review provides a comprehensive and mechanistic evaluation of E. crassipes valorization, moving beyond conventional assessments of biofuel production to examine the interconnected roles of pretreatment, inhibitor dynamics, microbial mitigation strategies, techno-economic performance, and biorefinery integration. A key novelty of this review is the transition from viewing lignocellulose-derived microbial inhibitory compounds solely as fermentation barriers to considering them as potential value-added intermediates through catalytic upgrading, biological detoxification, and bioelectrochemical conversion. Furthermore, organ-specific valorization strategies are critically assessed to maximize carbon recovery while minimizing unnecessary energy inputs. The review systematically compares biochemical conversion routes, including bioethanol, biohydrogen, biomethane, and biohythane production, and highlights the advantages of hybrid pretreatment technologies, syntrophic microbial consortia, and anaerobic co-digestion in enhancing process performance. In addition, techno-economic and commercialization challenges are critically discussed, with emphasis on minimum product selling price, carbon-credit integration, and scale-up feasibility. Finally, a future roadmap is proposed that combines digital-twin technologies, artificial intelligence, and integrated cascading biorefineries to transform E. crassipes from an invasive environmental burden into a climate-positive feedstock supporting circular bioeconomy and net-zero objectives.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jer.2026.10.005
