TY  - GEN
ID  - SG-38E0D1E41DB4
TI  - Effect of organic loading rate, carbon to nitrogen ratio and feeding strategy on the biosynthesis of tyrosine and tryptophan in aerobic granular sludge wastewater system
AU  - Mehdi Mohammadpour
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.24124/2025/30705
AB  - ,Aerobic granular sludge (AGS) was investigated as a dual-function platform for wastewater treatment and the recovery of tyrosine and tryptophan. Nine laboratory-scale experimental runs were conducted using sequencing batch bioreactors operated under a Taguchi design to evaluate the effects of organic loading rate (OLR: 0.8, 1.5 and 2.1 kg COD/m³·d), carbon-to-nitrogen (C/N) ratio (10:1, 20:1 and 30:1) and feeding regime (60 min slow feeding, 30 min feeding + 30 min resting and 10 min pulse feeding + 50 min resting). Treatment efficiency remained high, with COD removal ranging from 78 ± 2% to 94 ± 2%, while maximum NH₃-N and PO₄³⁻-P removals reached 97 ± 3% and 90 ± 0%, respectively. Stable granulation was maintained under all conditions, although extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) varied considerably, with protein ranging from 1 ± 0 to 133 ± 48 mg/g MLVSS and polysaccharide from 2 ± 0 to 79± 37 mg/g MLVSS. Tyrosine and tryptophan were consistently detected, with concentrations ranging from 0.2 ± 0.0 – 4.0 ± 0.5 mg/g MLVSS and 0.1 ± 0.0 – 0.7 ± 0.1 mg/g MLVSS, respectively. The highest concentrations were obtained at moderate OLR (1.5 kg COD/m³·d), low C/N (10:1) and pulsed feeding, where tyrosine peaked at 4.0 ± 0.5 mg/g MLVSS and tryptophan at 0.7 ± 0.1 mg/g MLVSS. Statistical analyses clarified the relative importance of operational factors. At the 95% confidence level, ANOVA identified OLR as a significant determinant of tyrosine concentration (p = 0.0167). Taguchi analysis also ranked OLR as the most influential factor, showing the largest delta values for both tyrosine (Δ = 1.72) and tryptophan (Δ = 0.25). C/N ratio was the second most influential, followed by feeding strategy, which had the smallest deltas. Although feeding strategy exerted a weaker effect, pulsed feeding consistently enhanced tyrosine and tryptophan yields compared with continuous or split regimes. From a practical perspective, these findings suggest that operating AGS under moderate OLR (≈1.5 kg COD/m³·d), low C/N ratio (10:1) and pulsed feeding can simultaneously maximize treatment efficiency and aromatic amino acid concentration. This positions AGS as a promising dual-purpose technology for Water Resource Recovery Facilities within circular bioeconomy frameworks. Nonetheless, as this work was conducted using synthetic wastewater, validation under real wastewater conditions remains necessary before pilot- or full-scale application.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.24124/2025/30705
