TY  - GEN
ID  - SG-D35E4EFB23C1
TI  - Electrochemistry Analytical Techniques and Interpretation of the Results
AU  - Sufia ul Haque
AU  - Mohammad Yasir
AU  - Narcis Duţeanu
PY  - 2023
DO  - 10.1002/9783527839001.ch2
AB  - Chemical processes connected to charge separation are called electrochemistry, and they often occur in liquid media. Charge transfer, which can take place either homogeneously in solution or heterogeneously on electrode surfaces, is frequently caused by charge separation. Two or more charge transfer half-reactions, oxidations (electrons lost or rise in oxidation status) and reductions (gain of electrons), occur concurrently and in opposite directions to ensure electroneutrality (gain of electrons or decrease in oxidation state). Heterogeneous redox reactions, which typically take place at various electrodes dipped in solution within a cell, are spatially isolated from one another. In order for charge to be carried and the electrical circuit to be completed, electrodes are connected by conducting routes both within the solution (through ionic transport) and externally (by electric wires). By-products of reactions at the two electrodes can be isolated if the cell arrangement allows it. The electrical energy released can be used when the total free energy changes at both electrodes are negative (batteries, fuel cells). If it is positive, it is possible to supply additional electrical energy to overcome the positive free energy, force electrode reactions to occur, and transform chemical compounds (electrolysis). This chapter discusses the fundamentals, techniques, and fields of application of electrochemistry.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1002/9783527839001.ch2
