TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-360E2FBEF982
TI  - Coupling Bioelectrochemical Systems and Redox Flow Batteries for Sustainable Energy Production and Storage
AU  - Márcia S. S. Santos
JO  - Portuguese National Funding Agency for Science, Research and Technology (RCAAP Project by FCT)
PY  - 2020
DO  - 10.34626/2kma-ka90
AB  - This thesis studies for the first time ever a combined system where Bioelectrochemical Systems (BESs) are used for charging electrochemical fuels used in Redox Flow Cells (RFCs); the coupled system was named Microbial Redox Flow Cell (MRFC).Using microorganisms as biocatalysts, BESs convert organic fuels (e.g.wastes) into electrochemical energy.RFCs use redox pairs dissolved in a supporting electrolyte that undergo reversible redox reactions during which chemical energy is either stored or released in the form of electricity.For the first time ever, a MRFC was studied using as redox pairs anthraquinone-2,6-disulfonate (2,6-AQDS)negalyteand ferrocyanide, ([Fe(CN)6] 4-)posilyte.The reduction of 2,6-AQDS to anthrahydroquinone-2,6-disulfonate (2,6-AQDSH2) in a BES was studied, where the Electrochemically Active Bacteria (EAB), Geobacter sulfurreducens and acetate were introduced in the anodic chamber.The anode and cathode chambers were physically separated with a Nafion™ NM 117 Proton Exchange Membrane.The reduction of 2,6-AQDS at the BES was studied first applying an external electrical bias of -0.200 V vs.Ag/AgCl (3 M) and then recording the electrical current history; secondly, the reduction of 2,6-AQDS was also obtained inserting an electrical resistance in the circuit, and measuring the potential difference at its ends.A BES loaded with 20 mM 2,6-AQDS aqueous solution in the cathodic chamber reached 3.3x10 -3 mW cm -2 (0.04 mA cm -2 ) with the BES closed with a 500 Ω resistor.In a second stage, the BES was operated with a 500 Ω resistor and reached a maximum of 0.05 mA cm -2 and 0.03 mW cm -2 , during the electrolyte charging.Studies with Ultraviolet-visible spectrophotometry (UV-Vis spectrophotometry) confirmed the presence of ca. 5 mM of 2,6-AQDSH2 at the cathode side, highlighting that the initial 2,6-AQDS molecules were successfully reduced.Because 2,6-AQDSH2 was produced following the bio-reduction that took place on the cathode side of the BES, this molecule was named as microbial electrochemical fuel.The 2,6-AQDSH2 bioelectrochemically reduced was then introduced in a 16 cm 2 RFC (negalyte) with ferricyanide, ([Fe(CN)6] 3-), (posilyteelectrically charged), and the RFC was allowed to discharge.The characteristic charge-discharge curve versus time was obtained, thus demonstrating the concept of a MRFC; a Coulombic Efficiency (CE) of ca. 100 % and Energy Efficiency (EE) ca.85 % over 10 cycles were obtained.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.34626/2kma-ka90
