TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-3FF8F4C5B74F
TI  - Kinetics and microbial ecology of chain elongation for production of medium-chain carboxylic acids
AU  - Pieter Candry
JO  - Ghent University Academic Bibliography (Ghent University)
PY  - 2020
AB  - Bioproduction of chemicals and energy from (agro)-industrial waste streams has the potential to become a key technology in a biobased economy.Chain elongation is a microbial process that can be used to produce medium-chain carboxylic acids (MCCA), attractive bioproducts for multiple reasons.Firstly, they can be produced using openculture microbiomes, resulting in stable and low-cost processes.Secondly, their physicochemical properties allow for easy separation during production.Lastly, their wide range of potential applications in food, feed, fuel and chemical industries, gives them a high market-value and the potential to turn wastes into profit.Within this thesis, an emphasis was placed on improving the understanding of the bacteria involved in this process and how to steer them to more efficiently produce these MCCA.A first approach was the development of a mathematical growth model for Clostridium kluyveri, a model organism for chain elongation.A high-throughput methodology was developed to assess the impact of substrates and products on growth rates (µ) of C. kluyveri during MCCA-production.Using this data to calibrate a kinetic growth model allowed determination of maximum growth rate (µmax; 0.24±0.01h -1 ), substrate affinity for acetic acid (KS,ac; 3.8±0.9mM) and inhibition concentrations of butyric acid (KI,but; 124.7±5.7 mM) and caproic acid (91.3±10.8mM).Coupling this kinetic model to a dynamic growth model allowed simulation of MCCA-production by C. kluvyeri.The deviations in the model predictions revealed knowledge gaps on intracellular and membrane-related processes were identified that could improve future models of MCCA-production by C. kluyveri.Open-culture microbiomes can lower the costs of a bioproduction process by removing sterilisation need.It is important to select a suitable inoculum and impose selective conditions to achieve the desired outcome.Chapter 3 investigated these aspects by enriching ethanol-utilising chain elongating microbiomes from manmade and natural environments at pH 7 and 5.5.This approach revealed that (i) ethanoldriven chain elongators were present in every environment, but the environment determined pH of chain elongator growth; (ii) microbiomes at low pH were simpler than those at neutral pH, with 96.9±1.2% of the community made up of a close relative of C. kluyveri; and (iii) final product concentrations were lower at pH 5.5, but growth rates were not affected by low pH.From these observations, it can be learned that inoculum selection is an important factor to consider during process design.Production of valeric acid (five-carbon carboxylic acid) by C. kluyveri can be used to better understand how substrates are used during chain elongation.The effect of the ratio of ethanol to propionic acid (the three-carbon carboxylic acid) on product selectivity and substrate efficiency was investigated.High ratios of ethanol:propionic acid induced more ethanol oxidation to acetic acid, and consequently, lower product selectivities.This oxidation of ethanol could not be mitigated by supplying additional H2, while kinetic experiments indicated that the competition between propionic acid and acetic acid as substrates is not well understood.Extending this knowledge shows that the metabolism of C. kluyveri is more flexible than previously thought, and the amount of ethanol dosed in a process will determine its outcome and cost balance.While ethanol is a suitable substrate for production of MCCA, it is a valuable product, and its use affects the cost balance and environmental impact.Instead, lactic acid derived from carbohydrates can be used to convert organic wastes to MCCA.Fermentation of lactic acid suffers from competition of propionic acid producers, lowering product selectivity.In Chapter 5, we confirmed literature observations that pH below 6 push the community towards chain elongation over propionic acid production, but, could demonstrate that this is the result of a combination of kinetic and thermodynamic advantages.Interfacing lactic acid-driven chain elongators with lactic acid-producers is a crucial step towards efficient MCCA-production processes from carbohydrate-rich wastes.Investigation of a fermentative granular biofilm-system harbouring both functional clades showed that these granular biofilms are made up of a handful of species.Mechanisms of granule formation and growth were investigated to understand the unique, lenticular morphology of these granular biofilms, which were shown to potentially be due to location-dependent growth rates of biofilm cells.This in turn could be caused by lower concentrations of caproic acid at the edges of the granular discs, demonstrating the importance of this parameter in the process.The wide range of MCCA-production processes investigated here shows that there is much potential for this platform to take hold in society, but, fundamental questions need to be answered to obtain efficient processes.What is the nature of the organisms involved?How can we efficiently steer and control them?What factors steer granulation?These fundamental questions can affect the process-level, the overall process design, and by extension, the feasibility of implementing these processes in real life.NEDERLANDSE SAMENVATTING Bioproductie van energie en chemische grondstoffen uit (agro)-industriële afvalstromen kan een sleuteltechnologie worden in de bio-economie.Ketenverlenging is een microbieel process voor de productie van middellange keten carbonzuren (MKCZ).Verscheidene eigenschappen maken deze MKCZ interessant.Ten eerste kunnen ze geproduceerd worden door open-cultuur microbiomen, wat zorgt voor een stabiel process met een lagere kost dan pure-cultuur processen.Ten tweede laten de fysicochemische eigenschappen van MKCZ toe om ze makkelijk af te scheiden tijdens het proces.Ten slotte zorgt hun brede waaier aan toepassingen in de voeder-, voedings-, brandstof-en chemische industrie voor een hoge marktwaarde, wat het mogelijk kan maken om winst te maken uit afvalstromen.In deze thesis werd de nadruk geplaatst op een beter begrip van de bacteriën betrokken in deze processen en hoe ze te sturen om efficiënter MKCZ te produceren.Een eerste aanpak was om een wiskundig groeimodel te ontwikkelen voor Clostridium kluyveri, een modelorganisme voor ketenverlenging.Een methodiek met hoge doorvoer werd ontwikkeld om de impact te bepalen van substraten en producten op de groeisnelheid (µ) van C. kluyveri.Met deze data werd een kinetisch groeimodel gekalibreerd, wat toeliet de de maximale groeisnelheid (µmax; 0.24±0.01h -1 ), affiniteit voor azijnzuur (KS,ac; 3.8±0.9mM) en inhibitie door boterzuur (KI,but; 124.7±5.7 mM) en capronzuur (91.3±10.8mM) te bepalen.Door dit kinetisch model aan een dynamisch groeimodel te koppelen werd het mogelijk MKCZ-productie door C. kluyveri te voorspellen.De afwijkingen in deze voorspellingen legden ontbrekende kennis bloot met betrekking tot intracellulaire en membraan-gerelateerde processen.Dergelijke kennis zou toekomstige modellen voor MCKZ-productie door C. kluyveri kunnen verbeteren.Open-cultuur microbiomen drukken de kosten van bioproductie, omdat er geen nood is aan sterilisatie.Om de gewenste functionaliteit te bekomen in dergelijke microbiomen is het belangrijk om een geschikt inoculum onder selectieve druk te plaatsen.Dit was de focus van Hoofdstuk 3, waar ethanol-consumerende ketenverlengende microbiomen werden aangerijkt uit natuurlijke en kunstmatige omgevingen bij pH 7 and 5.5.Deze aanpaak toonde aan dat (i) ethanol-consumerende ketenverlengers aanwezig waren in elke omgeving, maar, dat de omgeving de pH van groei bepaalde, (ii) microbiomen bij neutrale pH complexer waren dan die bij lage pH, waar een dichte verwant van C. kluyveri 96.9±1.2%vertegenwoordigde, en (iii) finale productconcentraties lager waren bij pH 5.5 maar groeisnelheden werden niet beïnvloed door de pH.
