TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-5908206630A6
TI  - Potentials and Future Outlook for BTEX and Produced Water Treatment using Buoyant Photocatalysts
AU  - Christopher Chan
JO  - TSpace (University of Toronto)
PY  - 2024
AB  - This thesis explores the applicability of using buoyant photocatalysts as a method of preventing gaseous benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes (BTEX) emissions from oil and gas wastewater ponds, which are harmful to environmental and human health. Current wastewater treatment methods are expensive, generate secondary waste streams, and have difficulty with organics. In this work, buoyant photocatalysts are explored as a passive in-situ treatment approach to prevent the volatilization of BTEX by forming a semi-permeable layer while simultaneously degrading them. They were shown to be able to reduce BTEX emissions by over 90% even within the presence of competing salt ions, and were capable of degrading additional dosages of BTEX added into the system, showing its applicability to real wastewater ponds. Kinetics and intermediates analysis offer mechanistic insights to BTEX oxidation. Suggestions for future applications into industrial processes such as reactor design and integration with other treatment methods were also investigated.
