PY  - 2011
AB  - Project backgroundRoad traffic is responsible for 28% of greenhouse gases (GHG) in Austria; it also causes levels of particulate matter (PM10, PM 2.5) and nitrogen dioxide (NO 2 ) to exceed statutory limits.Today, particulate matter (both PM10 and PM 2.5), NO 2 and PAH (Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons) are the most critical air pollutants in European cities, with a negative impact on people's health.The European limit values for PM10 and NO 2 cannot be met in many Austrian and European towns and cities.Moreover, many European regions fail to attain the new limit value for PM 2.5.In cities such as Klagenfurt (100 000 inhabitants) the limit value for the PM10 daily average is exceeded on up to 80 days per year.The most urgent problems regarding exhaust emissions from motor traffic are NOx and CO 2 emissions, whilst particulate emissions from new vehicles have already been brought down to very low levels.On the other hand, biomass with its low GHG load as of now only has a small share in vehicle fuels and bio-diesel has not solved the NOx problems.
