TI  - Archipelago of endolithic microbial life in the hyper arid core of the Atacama Desert
AU  - Jacek Wierzchoś
AU  - Alfonso F. Dávila
AU  - Jocelyne DiRuggiero
AU  - Asunción de los Rı́os
AU  - Sergio Valea
AU  - Beatriz Cámara Gallego
AU  - Octavio Artieda
AU  - Mariela Speranza
AU  - Courtney K. Robinson
AU  - T. Souterre
AU  - Virginia Souza‐Egipsy
AU  - Carmen Ascaso
JO  - DIGITAL.CSIC (Spanish National Research Council (CSIC))
PY  - 2012
AB  - The hyper-arid core of the Atacama Desert is considered the driest and most life-limited place on Earth. \nAs such, this desert is an ideal environment to study the survival and biological adaptation strategies of \nmicroorganisms to this extremely dry environment. The extreme aridity of the Atacama Desert allows us to \naddress questions such as the potential for life in extraterrestrial environments such as the surface of Mars. \nOur results over the last six years of searching for microbial life in the hyper-arid core of the Atacama Desert \nshows the presence of various habitats capable of sustaining active microbial ecosystems. All of them belong to \nendolithic – inside the rocks – microbial habitats. We suggest that these endolithic habitats protect microbial \ncommunities from UV and excessive PAR radiation and supply increased moisture, thus providing environmental \nrefuges – or islands of life - in the desert. This «archipelago» of microbial islands of life is composed by the \nfollowing endolitically colonized substrates: a) halite rocks (Wierzchos et al., 2006; de los Ríos et al., 2010); b) \ngypsum crusts (Wierzchos et al., 2011), c) volcanic origin ignimbrite (Wierzchos et al., 2012) and d) rhyolite \nand calcite rocks (DiRuggiero et al., submitted).
