AB  - The papers reviewed herein address surface water flow phenomena and fluid mechanics relating to the mixing and transport of pollutants in the environment.It is recognized that mixing and transport represents an extremely broad subject area, and other contributions may exist in addition to those listed here.Thus, this review is intended to present a cross-section of the recent work in this broad subject area.The review begins with topics on general mixing and fluid dynamics, and the general environmental fate and transport processes.Fluid dynamics and mixing topics include mixing and turbulence effects, jets and plumes, boundary layers, and mixing layers and stratified flows.Fate and exchange processes include watershed and source considerations, general environmental fate and transport, air-water exchanges and miscellaneous gas transfer processes, sediment characteristics and sediment transport processes, and miscellaneous biotic interactions.The remaining sections of this paper summarize research with specific applications in riverine, lacustrine, estuarine, and coastal and pelagic marine systems.GENERAL MIXING AND FLUID DYNAMICS Mixing and turbulence effects While many studies have been completed addressing mixing and turbulence, a few examples are presented here.Mocikat et al. (2003) completed laboratory 2025a
