TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-9404A5276F5C
TI  - Engineering biology and surface chemistry approaches to investigate the electroautotrophic potential of Cupriavidus necator H16
AU  - Francesco Catrambone
JO  - Nottingham ePrints (University of Nottingham)
PY  - 2022
AB  - Sustainable routes to building block chemicals are urgently needed if the ambitious target for the almost complete reduction of carbon emissions by 2035 is to be met. A potential disruptive technology that can contribute to this challenge is the advancement of microbial electrosynthesis (MES), using renewable energy sources and CO2 sourced from waste or atmospheric capture. However, the commercial viability of this platform is hindered by several limitations, like the low efficiency of electron transfer rates obtainable between the abiotic (cathode/anode) and biotic counterparts. To improve the efficiency of MES, both biological engineering of a biocatalyst - Cupriavidus necator H16 - and process design, were considered during this study. C. necator H16 is a model organism for autotrophic CO2 fermentation into industrially relevant compounds, therefore, an opportunity exists to engineer it for MES application. The primary aim of this work was to investigate the electroautotrophic potential of C. necator H16, using an interdisciplinary approach, with the objective of its employment in a scalable MES platform suitable for different environments and applications. To this end, through surface display of a SNAP-tag protein and a response surface methodology approach to engineering the properties of a mixed self-assembled monolayer (mSAM) deposited on gold, a covalent immobilisation strategy was obtained to control the spatial distribution of C. necator H16 on the surface of an electrode. Then, an attempt to engineer it with electroautotrophic properties by employing the exogenous Mtr cytochromes derived from Shewanella Oneidensis proved inconclusive, as no expression of recombinant proteins nor alteration of C. necator H16 electrophysiology was observed. However, the expression of recombinant ‘e-pili’ derived from Geobacter Metallireducens, indicated the mediation of an increase in electron flow between bacteria and electrode, suggesting a promising strategy for MES application. Finally, process optimisation was considered equally important to improve upon the results obtained so far in the literature, therefore a custom-designed, modular bio-electrochemical system (BES), along with a novel transition metal-doped electrode for in situ H2 electro catalysis, were employed to attempt the production of 2, 3-butanediol (2, 3-BDO) with C. necator H16. Despite the successful design and testing of the modular vessel, results indicated a decrease in long-term H2 electro-catalysis efficiency and the need of further investigations into the BES architecture. The results presented in this thesis present different opportunities for further research into the immobilisation of C. necator H16 as a biocatalyst. Moreover, recombinant e-pili expression suggests interesting prospects for future studies on bacterial electrophysiology and the application of C. necator H16 in MES. Additionally, during this work, several strategies have been identified to integrate key strengths and opportunities at the abiotic-biotic interface, allowing the production of industrially relevant compounds from CO2 in MES using C. necator H16 as a biocatalyst.
