TY  - GEN
ID  - SG-F23E0002E885
TI  - Formulation of a Biodegradable Controlled-Release Urea Fertiliser
AU  - Henri Le Sueur
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.26686/qb3z-zzh1
AB  - This research programme aimed to develop a controlled-release coated urea fertiliser that is biodegradable, non-toxic and had an effective urea release time on the order of 30-50 days at 100% water saturation, compared to uncoated urea which releases within minutes. Urea fertiliser is used extensively across New Zealand and the globe to boost agricultural and horticultural yields. Urea is applied in the form of a robust solid sphere called a prill. These prills have a diameter of 1-2 mm. Because of the very high solubility of urea prills in water (545 g/L) and the mobility of the nitrate ion in wet soils, excessive application of urea fertiliser leads to significant negative environmental and economic consequences through nitrogen losses to waterways and the atmosphere via nitrate leaching and ammonia and nitrous oxide volatilisation respectively. Controlled-release coatings around the urea prills can be used to control the extent of dissolved urea entering and overloading the soil, significantly reducing the negative environmental effects of urea use. This reduces the losses of nitrogen through volatilisation and leaching, significantly decreasing aquatic pollution and greenhouse gas emissions. Current controlled-release coatings have not realised widespread use in the agricultural industry, however, because they are typically expensive and made from toxic and non- biodegradable materials. The option of a non-toxic, biodegradable and affordable controlled-release urea fertiliser that has an effective urea release period is simply not available. Therefore, the vast majority of nitrogen fertiliser applied today is plain uncoated urea which dissolves within minutes, leading to the negative effects described. This research programme focused on developing a controlled-release coating for urea fertiliser that is economical to use, bio-degradable, non-toxic, and has a significantly slower urea delivery rate than uncoated urea (on the order of 30-50 days). Following a detailed literature survey, cellulose was identified as a basic foundational material, with several derivatives, including hydroxyethyl cellulose and cellulose acetate butyrate, found to have potential as coatings. Cellulose is a naturally produced, highly abundant, low cost, non-toxic and biodegradable material. Hydroxyethyl cellulose cross-linked with citric acid in water was found to form mostly insoluble films, however these suffered from a high gel fraction of about 10%. They also had a swelling ratio of about 10% (w/w) water. Attempts to coat urea with hydroxyethyl cellulose using a prototype rotary coating mechanism encompassing a benchtop rotary evaporator were hindered by the inherent stickiness of the polymer, with coating attempts either causing agglomeration or dissolution of the urea. Manual dip coating was attempted but it was found that there was a low affinity between the urea and the polymer coating after drying. Cellulose acetate butyrate in acetone formed insoluble and highly hydrophobic films. These films had no gel fraction and a low swelling rate of 2-3% (w/w) water. Urea prills were successfully coated with cellulose acetate butyrate using a laborious manual dip method to negate the stickiness problem. Each urea prill was dipped into the coating solution using tweezers before being dried on baking paper. This coating process was repeated to gain thicker coatings. Water immersion urea release tests were used as a benchmark to compare each controlled-release coating against uncoated urea. It is important to note that these tests are at 100% water saturation, a far higher water content than on a typical pasture. This means that the release of urea from dissolution of the prill in pastures will be slower than with the benchmark water immersion tests. The initial tests showed that with seven coatings of cellulose acetate butyrate, the coated-urea released their urea content over two days in a diffusion controlled release, as shown by its exponential curve. This is compared to less than one hour with uncoated urea. Increasing the concentration of cellulose acetate butyrate from 10 to 20 weight percent did not lead to a significant increase in release time and made the surface morphology of the coatings poorer. Fillers including nanostructured calcium silicate, kaolinite and montmorillonite were added at 2.5, 5 and 10 weight percent relative to the polymer weight. These fillers successfully enhanced the barrier properties of the coatings by increasing the tortuosity of diffusion. This effort extended the release time of urea to eight days. A dual-layered coating system was developed using cellulose acetate butyrate + hydrophobic palmitic acid as interior and exterior layers respectively and successfully extended the urea release time to over 20 days. Finally, a triple-layered coating system consisting of cellulose acetate butyrate + palmitic acid + cellulose acetate butyrate managed to extend the release time of urea to 50 days and significantly flatten the diffusion curve, giving a more constant release of urea than is typically observed in diffusion-controlled release. Pot plant trials growing a mixture of ryegrass and white clover were performed using the dual and triple-layered coated urea and were tested against a control group with uncoated urea and a blank with no added fertiliser. These tests showed that both coated urea samples gave a greater dry matter production similar than uncoated urea after 12 weeks of growth, demonstrating that the coated fertiliser closely matches the nutrient demand of the plants. The differences between the double and triple-coated urea were not significant after 12 weeks, however the faster releasing double-coated urea was still producing the most growth, suggesting that with a longer trial the triple-coated urea may show greater growth after 16-24 weeks. This research programme resulted in the successful development of a non-toxic, biodegradable, and affordable controlled-release coating for urea fertiliser with a release time of 50 days while in fully wet conditions. This coated urea could eliminate approximately 1% of New Zealand’s annual greenhouse gas emissions by reducing nitrous oxide production. It will also significantly reduce nitrate leaching into aquatic environments and have the added benefit of saving farmers money by increasing the use efficiency of urea. This technology demonstrates a solution to a significant problem and is key step towards a sustainable agricultural industry both in New Zealand and globally.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.26686/qb3z-zzh1
