TI  - Three decades of gastroenterology in Soweto South Africa: from descriptive to scientific observations
AU  - I. Segal
JO  - UvA-DARE (University of Amsterdam)
PY  - 2002
AB  - Thiss section comprises 21 publications .The studies focus on disorders of the oesophagus,, stomach, duodenum, small and large intestine and biliary system.6 publications onn the urban experience are included.The articles describe the disease patterns and emergencee of Western chronic digestive diseases in the urban Black.In addition, occurrences,, characteristics, clinical manifestations and aetiological associations of the above disorderss are discussed.This section forms a database for the development of the thesis. B.. Intestinal disease conundrumInn Soweto non-infectious large bowel diseases have very low incidences despite changes in lifee style, dietary and non-dietary (page 7 -8).Risk factors favouring an increase in bowel diseasess include Westernization of diet with a decreasing intake of fibre containing foods, and changess in bowel function.Thus stool weights, defecation frequencies, oro-caecal transit timess and whole gut transit times in Sowetans approximate those of Western populations.Factorss believed to be inhibiting a rise in bowel disease are physiological malabsorption of lactose,, maize, fructose.Sucrase activity is significantly lower compared with Whites.There aree also differences in colon cell proliferation, faecal short chain fatty acids, faecal pH and colonicc microflora compared to White groups.AA hypothesis to explain the persistent low prevalence of Western digestive diseases in Africa hass been proposed.It is suggested that the critical events which occur in early infancy lay the foundationss for a healthy gastrointestinal tract which can withstand insults that may occur in laterr life.Central to this hypothesis is the quality of the intestinal bacteria, the integrity of the Summary yAA low residue, fiber-deficient diet has been postulated as the cause of diverticular disease -the commonestt affliction of the colon in the Western nations.This condition is virtually unknown inn Africans (blacks) who live south of the Sahara, and who have not changed their traditional highh residue diet.This paper presents 16 Africans with diverticular disease.A disturbing featuree is that 5 of the patients are young, being in their fourth decade, and only 4 are over the agee of 60.Their dietary history confirms the hypothesis as to the etiology, and also shows that thee urbanization of the South African black has been accompanied by the emergence of diverticularr disease, a condition hitherto virtually nonexistent in this population. Summary yInn Western communities diverticular disease occurs mainly in the sigmoid colon.This contrastss with Oriental populations, in which diverticula occur mainly in the right colon.Diverticularr disease has recently emerged in black South Africans.This study shows that diverticulaa in this population occurs predominantly in the descending colon.The variable anatomicc distribution of diverticula in different ethnic groups implies that fiber deficiency is nott the only factor responsible for this condition.It is suggested that diverticular disease may comprisee several entities with different causes. Summary yPrevalencess of non-infective bowel diseases are very low in South African urban blacks comparedd with the white population.In seeking elucidation, using breath hydrogen measurementss in series of black and white subjects, small-bowel transit time was determined, andd the malabsorption of maize, wheat, and rice investigated.Median transit times in both ethnicc groups were similar.Rice was fully, but wheat incompletely absorbed by both groups.Maize,, the staple food of blacks, was incompletely absorbed by them, although completely absorbedd by the white subjects.Carbohydrate consumption is high in the black population (60 --65% of total energy intake).It is probable that in blacks, despite their now eating a low-fibre diet,, an expected increase in large-bowel diseases has been inhibited in part by the protective mechanismm of fermentation of malabsorbed maize and wheat. Summary yDiminishedd levels for fecal short chain fatty acids (SCFAs) have been linked to occurrence of ulcerativee colitis, colorectal polyps, and colon cancer, diseases that are rare or uncommon in Africann populations.Purpose: The aim of this study was to determine fecal SCFA concentrationss and fecal pH values in groups of black South Africans (African) and white Southh Africans (white) subjects.Methods: Twenty healthy Africans (all women; mean age, 35 years)) and 17 healthy whites (7 women; 10 men; mean age, 32 years) were tested.Results:Meann total concentrations of SCFAs in the two groups were 142.1 (+/S3.9)and 69.2 (+/-26.0))mmol/kg wet feces, respectively (P -0.0001).Mean values for Africans were significantlyy higher in all subfractions except butyratc.There was a significant inverse correlationn between fecal pH value and total fecal SCFA concentration (r = 0.704; P = 0.001).Conclusion:: High concentrations of fecal SCFAs in the African group could protect against chronicc bowel diseases. Summary yThiss study reflects the fact that cancer of the large bowel is uncommon in South African Blacks,, and that colorectal polyps do not appear to constitute a precursor to most colorectal cancers.. Furthermore it is shown that dietary factors associated with this cancer in Western populationss are not evident in the Black population.In the South African White population, however,, the disease behaves in a similar way to that observed in Western countries.Other definitivee differences found were the absence of multiple synchronous cancers and diverticularr disease in the Blacks with colorectal cancer.It is thus postulated that dietary factorss are absent, or have not been present for a sufficient length of time to influence the developmentt of polyps or polyp-cancer sequence in this population.It is also possible that the adenoma-carcinomaa progression observed in Western countries may not be relevant to the developmentt of all colorectal carcinomas in communities such as those reported here.
