TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-44793C2DEAE0
TI  - Carbon monoxide adaptation and utilisation in Cupriavidus necator
AU  - Charles Wickham-Smith
JO  - Nottingham ePrints (University of Nottingham)
PY  - 2020
AB  - The C1 gas carbon monoxide (CO) is abundantly present in industrial waste gases, which can be used as a feedstock for the biological production of industrially significant chemicals and fuels. Fermentation with such CO-rich gases may form a vital part of the strategy to reduce the levels of greenhouse gases in the Earth’s atmosphere, which are damaging to the environment. For efficient bacterial growth and to increase product yield in the presence of high CO concentrations, resistance to the gas and its utilisation are required. The aerobic C1 chassis Cupriavidus necator H16 is able to utilise CO2 + H2, although cannot use CO as a sole source of carbon and energy and is inhibited by the gas. To increase the tolerance of C. necator to CO, the organism was continually subcultured in the presence of CO. Ten individual cultures were evolved in this manner and, after 15 subcultures, displayed a clear growth advantage when compared to the wild type strain. Following whole genome sequencing of single isolates from these cultures, a number of unique mutations were found, including a single point mutation upstream of a cytochrome bd unbiquinol oxidase operon (cydA2B2), which was present in all ten isolates. When a subset of these mutations was engineered into the genome of the original parental strain, only the upstream cydA2B2 mutation enabled the fast growing phenotype in the presence of CO. Characterisation of this mutation and the cydA2B2 operon through gene expression analysis, mutation and overexpression suggested that cytochrome bd production is upregulated in the evolved isolates, resulting in increased CO tolerance. Utilisation of CO requires CO dehydrogenases (CODHs), which are found in various chemolithoautorophic organisms, which, in contrast to C. necator, harbour functional CODH-encoding cox gene clusters. To equip C. necator with the required set of genes, transfer of the Oligotropha carboxidovorans pHCG3 megaplasmid was investigated, as it contains one of the best characterised cox gene cluster and a quorum sensing (QS) system assumed to play a role in regulating conjugal transfer. To optimise pHCG3 transfer, the putative QS repressor TraM was inactivated, enabling constitutive N-acyl-L-homoserine lactone production, boosting the conjugative functions of pHCG3. Transfer into plasmid-less strains of Agrobacterium tumefaciens was achieved, enabling AHL production in this organism, but not the ability to utilise CO or CO2 + H2. A novel species of Pseudomonas was accidentally isolated whilst carrying out conjugation experiments, which stably maintained this pHCG3 derivative, although the plasmid appeared to be non functional. pHCG3 could not be successfully transferred into C. necator, presumably due to species specific barriers such as restriction systems, plasmids instability, host immunity or plasmid incompatibility.
