TY  - GEN
ID  - SG-8D0792DA9F08
TI  - Responses of Sulfate-Reducing Microorganisms to Biocide Treatments under Planktonic and Biofilm Conditions
AU  - A. Walker
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.11575/prism/51664
AB  - The souring and corrosion of petroleum-associated systems pose substantial operational and environmental challenges. Sulfate-reducing microorganisms (SRM), abundant in these habitats, are major contributors to these processes through the production of sulfide, a toxic byproduct of their metabolism, posing risk to human health while simultaneously driving souring and microbiologically influenced corrosion. Biocide treatment represents the most widely employed strategy for controlling SRM activity in these settings; however their efficacy is limited by the presence of biofilm structures, which demonstrate increased ability to withstand chemical treatment in comparison to their planktonic counterparts. The mechanism of this tolerance remains poorly understood, especially in mixed SRM communities. Here, SRM susceptibility to the biocide ADBAC (alkyldimethylbenzylammonium chloride) is the focus of investigation, with results demonstrating at least a 10-fold higher concentration of ADBAC required to inhibit the activity of biofilms compared to planktonic communities. To investigate the mechanisms of tolerance, a corrosive, highly enriched SRM culture was exposed to a sublethal ADBAC concentration (20 ppm) in a planktonic setting. Meta-transcriptomic analysis suggested a variety of tolerance mechanisms, such as those involved in energy pathways, chemotaxis and motility, stress regulation, and efflux pumps. These aligned closely with the predicted metagenomic potential, especially with use of efflux pumps. These findings provide one of the first characterizations of tolerance associated responses from a metagenomic and meta-transcriptional perspective in a mixed SRM community. By furthering this understanding of biocide tolerance mechanisms in petroleum-associated SRM communities, more informed decisions can be made regarding treatment strategies to mitigate microbial souring and corrosion in oilfield systems.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.11575/prism/51664
