AB  - performance of a membrane bioreactor (MBR) for denitrification of nitrate (NO 3 -) contaminated drinking water.The results indicated a 99% NO 3 -removal at an influent concentration of 200 mg/L NO 3 -N and low effluent turbidity.Tsal et al. (2004) developed a mathematical model for performance prediction, simulation, and design of membrane bioreactor (MBR) process for biological denitrification of two groundwaters.Model sensitivity studies illustrated the dependence of process dynamics on parameters related to influent nitrate concentration, biomass concentration, biodegradation kinetics, and hydraulic residence time.MEMBRANE FOULING Membrane fouling has continued to be a major area of research.Sofia et al. (2004) discussed the engineering approaches for minimum fouling in submerged MBR.The author observed that membrane cleaning by air scour at critical aeration intensity was able to prolong the membrane operation for up to 8 months without needing chemical and mechanical cleaning.Kim et al. (2004a) studied the influence of membrane surface properties on fouling in a membrane bioreactor for wastewater treatments.These results suggested that useful reduction in membrane fouling can be achieved by the control of pore size using polyvinylpyrrolidinone (PVP) as well as induction of hydrophility on the membrane surface by chemical cross-linking.The identification and quantification of foulants in membrane bioreactors present a major challenge due to their complexity resulting from biomass heterogeneity.Jefferson et al. (2004a) reviewed the available methods for quantifying and analysis fouling with specific reference to recent data on sub-critical flux behavior.It was 15547531, 2005, 6, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.
