TY  - JOUR
ID  - SG-0A3474BA5AF2
TI  - Process-yield-structure-bioconversion of Organosolv Lignin from Wheat straw
AU  - Nasim Ramezani
JO  - TSpace (University of Toronto)
PY  - 2019
AB  - The goal of this study is to present a cohesive process that embraces separation and reaction steps for producing small molecules from organosolv lignin with white-rot fungus. This study contains novel findings on 1) the ability of B. adusta to produce intracellular laccase, 2) the comprehensive structural analysis of lignin molecules and 3) new results on lignin breakdown to small molecules after treatment by the fungus. For this purpose, the extraction of organosolv lignin in optimum operational conditions has been carried out in a reactor. After extraction without any catalysts, all the green solvents (ethanol/water) were recovered and reused. The highest yield of lignin (90%) was obtained under optimal conditions (120 min, 200 °C, and 400 psi). The extracted lignin also contained the lowest amount of carbohydrate impurities and ash. Furthermore, a detailed approach of the characterization of extracted lignin has been conducted by FTIR, NMR, TGA, DSC, SEC and MALDI-TOF techniques. The characterizations led to the identification of side chain moieties, vanillin, phenylcoumaran and others. The presence of different moieties such as Cγ–O group β-O-4 units, added value to the extracted lignin. Likewise, the ability of B. adusta to bio-transform extracted lignin into small molecules was evaluated. Lignin peroxidase, manganese peroxidase, laccase and versatile peroxidase had the main role in the biodegradation of lignin to small fractions such as syringylglycerol-B-guaiacyl ether, sinapyl alcohol diacetate and others. As a result, the extracted lignin after treatment with B. adusta showed significant changes in its physical and chemical structure, which makes it a novel candidate for yielding value-added products.
