JO  - Biomass and Bioenergy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.biombioe.2026.110198
AB  - The environmental contamination and global warming, directly linked to the extraction and use of fossil fuels, have motivated the search for greener sources able to provide similar products (e.g. plastics, fuels, etc.). The research and development of biorefineries, where otherwise wasted biomass is used as a feedstock to produce valuable compounds and/or energy, is currently a trending topic in the scientific community. In fact, research into the potential use of seaweed within a circular economy model has increased exponentially over the last decade. However, scaling up the process to viable industrial plants currently has several drawbacks that need to be further understood and investigated, such as the development of economical drying processes to reduce transport costs, the optimization of specific extraction processes and the impact of compositional variations between batches, etc. Moreover, the potential use of the extracted biomass as feedstock for energy production (biohydrogen, biomethane, etc.) is poorly investigated in the literature. This review focuses, for the first time, on the production of biogas by anaerobic digestion as a key step in a comprehensive seaweed cascade valorisation process. Factors influencing the composition of marine algae, extraction methods and approaches for obtaining bioactive compounds are comprehensively presented and discussed. The impact of different parameters on the anaerobic digestion performance and their relationship with extracted algal residues are used for further discussion on scaled-up systems and their economic feasibility. Finally, possible future research and trends are proposed and discussed.
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biombioe.2026.110198
